// ---- Case files (t-cases): data, generated by src/cases/build_cases.py from inputs/extracts_*.json and annot_*.py. Do not edit by hand. ----
window.CF_DATA={"blocks":[{"k":"lb","name":"Load balancing and scaling out","def_":"Spread requests over many identical servers, and add servers as load grows."},{"k":"cache","name":"Caching","def_":"Keep a copy of an answer close by so you do not fetch or compute it again."},{"k":"repl","name":"Replication and consistency","def_":"Keep copies of data on several machines; decide what happens when the copies disagree."},{"k":"shard","name":"Sharding","def_":"Split one database's rows across several machines by a key, such as user or workspace."},{"k":"queue","name":"Queues and backpressure","def_":"Put work in a line for workers, and push back on senders when the line gets too long."},{"k":"retry","name":"Timeouts, retries and idempotency","def_":"Give up after a set wait, try again with growing random gaps, and make repeats harmless."},{"k":"degrade","name":"Circuit breakers and degradation","def_":"Fail small: cut off a sick dependency, shed load, and serve a reduced product."},{"k":"deploy","name":"Deployment and change safety","def_":"Roll changes (code and config) out gradually, watch health, roll back fast."},{"k":"obs","name":"SLOs and observability","def_":"Agree how reliable to be, and see what the system is doing, from outside it."},{"k":"gpu","name":"GPU serving","def_":"Run models: batching, KV cache, memory, routing requests to the right pool."}],"cases":[{"id":"cf-2026-05","org":"Cloudflare","type":"architecture","title":"After two outages: config changes now roll out like code","blocks":["deploy"],"flagship":false,"date":"2026-05-01","year":2026,"dateText":"1 May 2026","system":"Cloudflare's response to the November and December 2025 outages, a programme it called Code Orange: Fail Small.","happened":"Both 2025 outages were config changes pushed to the whole network in seconds, while software releases already went out gradually. Cloudflare built Snapstone, which packages any unit of configuration and releases it progressively, watching health metrics and rolling back automatically.","fix":"Health-mediated deployment for configuration by default. Cloudflare says the November bot-file change would now be caught while it affected only a small percentage of traffic.","lesson":"Treat a config push as a deploy: progressive rollout, health checks, automatic rollback.","note":null,"numbers":[],"terms":[["health-mediated deployment","release to a small slice, watch error rates, continue only if healthy, roll back automatically if not"],["progressive rollout","1% of servers, then 10%, then everything, with a pause to check between steps"]],"source":"Cloudflare blog, Code Orange: Fail Small is complete (Jeremy Hartman)","url":"https://blog.cloudflare.com/code-orange-fail-small-complete/","url2":null,"published":"2026-05-01","publishedText":"1 May 2026","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Central to this is a new internal component we call Snapstone, which we built to bring health-mediated deployment to configuration changes.","Snapstone closes this gap by providing a unified way to bring progressive rollout, real-time health monitoring, and automated rollback to configuration deployments by default.","As a result, if the same Bot Management change that caused the failure in November were to roll out now, the system would detect the failure in an early stage of the deployment, before it had affected anything more than a small percentage of traffic.","While improving resiliency will never be a “job done” and will always be a top priority across our development lifecycle, we have now completed the work that would have avoided the November 18, 2025 and December 5, 2025 global outages."]},{"id":"gh-2026-04-lb","org":"GitHub","type":"outage","title":"Scrapers saturated the load balancers in front of search","blocks":["lb","degrade"],"flagship":false,"date":"2026-04-27","year":2026,"dateText":"27 Apr 2026","system":"GitHub search sits behind its own tier of load balancers, and public API calls are rate limited per caller.","happened":"Anonymous scrapers spread their requests over more than 600,000 IP addresses so that no single address hit the rate limit. Their traffic was 30% of the day's search traffic, packed into four hours, and saturated the load balancers. Issues, pull requests, Actions and other features that rely on search saw up to 65% of searches time out or fail.","fix":"Scaled the load-balancer tier, blocked the traffic, tuned connection handling and reuse, and added controls to restrict anonymous traffic so signed-in users are protected.","lesson":"A per-caller rate limit fails against callers who split themselves; protect the shared tier with limits by class of user too.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"600,000","t":"unique IP addresses","q":4},{"v":"30%","t":"of the day's search traffic, in four hours","q":3},{"v":"65%","t":"peak share of searches failing for some services","q":1}],"terms":[["saturation","a resource is at 100% of its capacity, so new work queues or is refused"],["load balancer","the machine that receives requests and hands each to one of many servers behind it"]],"source":"GitHub blog, GitHub availability report: April 2026 (incident of April 27)","url":"https://github.blog/news-insights/company-news/github-availability-report-april-2026/","url2":null,"published":"2026-05-14","publishedText":"14 May 2026","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["On April 27, 2026, between 16:15 UTC and 22:46 UTC, GitHub search services experienced degraded connectivity due to saturation of the load balancing tier deployed in front of our search infrastructure.","The impact was varied by search target, with services seeing up to 65% of searches timing out or returning an error between 16:15 UTC and 18:00 UTC.","The saturation was caused by a large influx of anonymous distributed scraping traffic that was crafted to avoid our public API rate limits.","This scraping traffic made up 30% of the day's total search traffic, but it was concentrated within a four-hour period.","The traffic originated from over 600,000 Unique IP addresses, with matching actor information in all requests.","We've also added new monitors and controls within the platform to allow us to restrict anonymous traffic to mitigate the impact to our registered users."]},{"id":"gh-2026-04-dns","org":"GitHub","type":"outage","title":"DNS failed in one data center; caches kept most of GitHub up","blocks":["cache","lb"],"flagship":false,"date":"2026-04-23","year":2026,"dateText":"23 Apr 2026","system":"Inside a data center, GitHub's services find each other by name through internal DNS resolvers. A new traffic-balancing mechanism had been rolled out progressively to spread load.","happened":"Under one load pattern the new mechanism made the DNS resolvers in one data center fail intermittently. Every service that needed to look up an address (internal APIs, storage, outside providers) started failing at once. Services that had the address cached kept working, which is why only part of the traffic failed.","fix":"A config rollback did not help; restarting the DNS infrastructure did, within minutes. GitHub is hardening DNS so one data center cannot cascade, testing infrastructure changes against production-like traffic, and reviewing which services share it.","lesson":"A cache is also a shock absorber: it turned a full outage into a partial one.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"5–7%","t":"share of all traffic affected","q":0},{"v":"1 hour and 27 minute","t":"impact window, 23 Apr 2026","q":0}],"terms":[["resolver","the server a machine asks to turn a name into an address"],["cascade","one failure causing failures in everything that depends on it, and so on"]],"source":"GitHub blog, GitHub availability report: April 2026 (incident of April 23)","url":"https://github.blog/news-insights/company-news/github-availability-report-april-2026/","url2":null,"published":"2026-05-14","publishedText":"14 May 2026","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Approximately 5–7% of overall traffic was affected during the 1 hour and 27 minute impact window.","Our DNS infrastructure in one datacenter entered a degraded state and began intermittently failing to resolve service addresses.","The root cause was a recently introduced traffic-balancing mechanism that had been rolled out progressively to support our growth.","Existing DNS caching provided partial protection.","Services with recently cached entries continued operating normally, which is why overall impact was limited to approximately 5–7% of traffic rather than a complete outage.","After an initial configuration rollback did not resolve the issue, we restarted the affected DNS infrastructure."]},{"id":"cf-2026-02","org":"Cloudflare","type":"outage","title":"An empty flag meant \"all\": a cleanup job deleted customers' routes","blocks":["deploy","retry"],"flagship":false,"date":"2026-02-20","year":2026,"dateText":"20 Feb 2026","system":"Customers can bring their own IP address ranges (BYOIP) to Cloudflare, which announces them to the internet with BGP. A new automated cleanup task asked an internal API for prefixes pending deletion.","happened":"The task sent the flag pending_delete with no value. The server read an empty string, took that as \"no filter\", and returned every BYOIP prefix. The task then started withdrawing and deleting all of them until an engineer noticed and stopped it.","fix":"Reverted the change, re-announced prefixes (800 restored in one step), and gave customers a way to re-advertise themselves. Follow-ups: a typed API schema for such flags, and monitoring that pauses deployments when withdrawals happen too fast or too broadly.","lesson":"Destructive operations need a blast-radius brake: refuse or pause when a job suddenly touches far more than usual.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"1,100","t":"prefixes withdrawn","q":2},{"v":"6,500","t":"BYOIP prefixes in total","q":2},{"v":"17%","t":"share withdrawn","f":"1,100 / 6,500"},{"v":"50 min","t":"withdrawal window","f":"18:46 minus 17:56 UTC"}],"terms":[["BGP","the protocol networks use to tell the rest of the internet \"send traffic for these addresses to me\""],["prefix","a block of IP addresses announced together"]],"source":"Cloudflare blog, Cloudflare outage on February 20, 2026 (David Tuber, Dzevad Trumic)","url":"https://blog.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-outage-february-20-2026/","url2":null,"published":"2026-02-21","publishedText":"21 Feb 2026","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Because the client is passing pending_delete with no value, the result of Query().Get(“pending_delete”) here will be an empty string (“”), so the API server interprets this as a request for all BYOIP prefixes instead of just those prefixes that were supposed to be removed.","The new sub-task then began systematically deleting all BYOIP prefixes and all of their related dependent objects including service bindings , until the impact was noticed, and an engineer identified the sub-task and shut it down.","During the incident, 1,100 prefixes out of the total 6,500 were withdrawn from 17:56 to 18:46 UTC.","We will improve our monitoring to detect when changes are happening too fast or too broadly, such as withdrawing or deleting BGP prefixes quickly, and disable the deployment of snapshots when this happens.","Cloudflare was able to revert many of the advertisement changes around 20:20 UTC, which caused 800 prefixes to be restored."]},{"id":"cf-2025-11","org":"Cloudflare","type":"outage","title":"A config file that doubled in size crashed the proxy worldwide","blocks":["deploy","degrade"],"flagship":false,"date":"2025-11-18","year":2025,"dateText":"18 Nov 2025","system":"Cloudflare's proxy sits in front of millions of websites. Its bot-detection module loads a feature file (the inputs for a machine-learning model) that is regenerated every five minutes and pushed to every server in the world.","happened":"A permissions change to the ClickHouse database that builds the file made a query return duplicate rows, so the file doubled. The proxy had a hard limit of 200 features (normal use was about 60); the bigger file broke it, and the Rust code treated the error as impossible and crashed (panicked), serving 5xx errors. Because the database was being updated node by node, good and bad files alternated every five minutes, which made it look like an attack.","fix":"Cloudflare stopped the bad file, pushed a known-good one and restarted services. Its follow-ups: treat its own generated config like untrusted user input, add global kill switches per feature, and (see the 2026 card) roll config out gradually with health checks.","lesson":"Internal config is input too: validate it, roll it out in stages, and fail to the last good version instead of crashing.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"11:20","t":"failures begin (UTC, 18 Nov 2025)","q":0},{"v":"200","t":"feature limit in the proxy","q":3},{"v":"~60","t":"features in normal use","q":3},{"v":"14:30","t":"core traffic largely normal","q":6},{"v":"17:06","t":"all systems normal","q":7}],"terms":[["5xx","the family of HTTP status codes that mean the server failed (500, 502, 503 ...)"],["panic","the program stops itself because it hit a state its author assumed could not happen"],["kill switch","a flag that turns one feature off everywhere at once, without a new deploy"]],"source":"Cloudflare blog, Cloudflare outage on November 18, 2025 (Matthew Prince)","url":"https://blog.cloudflare.com/18-november-2025-outage/","url2":null,"published":"2025-11-18","publishedText":"18 Nov 2025","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["On 18 November 2025 at 11:20 UTC (all times in this blog are UTC), Cloudflare's network began experiencing significant failures to deliver core network traffic.","Instead, it was triggered by a change to one of our database systems' permissions which caused the database to output multiple entries into a “feature file” used by our Bot Management system.","That feature file, in turn, doubled in size.","Currently that limit is set to 200, well above our current use of ~60 features.","When the bad file with more than 200 features was propagated to our servers, this limit was hit -- resulting in the system panicking.","As a result, every five minutes there was a chance of either a good or a bad set of configuration files being generated and rapidly propagated across the network.","Core traffic was largely flowing as normal by 14:30.","As of 17:06 all systems at Cloudflare were functioning as normal.","Hardening ingestion of Cloudflare-generated configuration files in the same way we would for user-generated input","Enabling more global kill switches for features","Today was Cloudflare's worst outage since 2019","We solved the problem by stopping the generation and propagation of the bad feature file and manually inserting a known good file into the feature file distribution queue."]},{"id":"aws-2025-dwfm","org":"AWS","type":"outage","title":"EC2 could not hand out servers: a queue that never drained","blocks":["queue","retry"],"flagship":true,"date":"2025-10-20","year":2025,"dateText":"20 Oct 2025","system":"EC2 rents out virtual machines that run on physical servers AWS calls droplets. A manager service (DWFM) keeps a lease, a short-lived claim it must renew, on every droplet; a droplet with no lease cannot take new virtual machines.","happened":"DWFM's regular check-ins with droplets depend on DynamoDB. While DynamoDB was down (see the DNS card from the same night) those checks failed and leases slowly timed out. When DynamoDB came back at 2:25 AM, every droplet needed a new lease at once. Each attempt now waited so long in line that it timed out before it finished, and each timeout put a fresh attempt back in the line. The line never got shorter: AWS calls this congestive collapse. Customers asking for new machines got \"insufficient capacity\" errors.","fix":"At 4:14 AM engineers throttled incoming work (let in only as much as could finish) and restarted DWFM hosts, which emptied the queues; every droplet had a lease by 5:28 AM. AWS says it will rate limit incoming work by the size of the waiting queue and test this recovery path at scale.","lesson":"If work waits longer than its timeout, adding retries adds only waste: cap the queue, not just the workers.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"2:25 AM","t":"DynamoDB back; lease rebuilding starts (PDT, 20 Oct 2025)","q":1},{"v":"4:14 AM","t":"throttle plus restarts begin","q":6},{"v":"5:28 AM","t":"all droplets leased again","q":8},{"v":"3 h 3 min","t":"from start of rebuilding to all leased","f":"5:28 AM minus 2:25 AM"}],"terms":[["lease","a claim that expires unless it is renewed, so a crashed owner cannot hold a resource forever"],["throttle","deliberately let in less work than is offered, so the work you accept can finish"],["congestive collapse","the state where the system is fully busy but almost nothing useful completes"]],"source":"AWS, Summary of the Amazon DynamoDB Service Disruption in the Northern Virginia (US-EAST-1) Region (EC2 section)","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/101925/","url2":null,"published":"2025-10","publishedText":"Oct 2025","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Each DWFM manages a set of droplets within each Availability Zone and maintains a lease for each droplet currently under management.","At 2:25 AM PDT, with the recovery of the DynamoDB APIs, DWFM began to re-establish leases with droplets across the EC2 fleet.","DWFM began the process of reestablishing leases with droplets across the EC2 fleet; however, due to the large number of droplets, efforts to establish new droplet leases took long enough that the work could not be completed before they timed out.","Additional work was queued to reattempt establishing the droplet lease.","At this point, DWFM had entered a state of congestive collapse and was unable to make forward progress in recovering droplet leases.","Since any droplet without an active lease is not considered a candidate for new EC2 launches, the EC2 APIs were returning “insufficient capacity errors” for new incoming EC2 launch requests.","After attempting multiple mitigation steps, at 4:14 AM engineers throttled incoming work and began selective restarts of DWFM hosts to recover from this situation.","Restarting the DWFM hosts cleared out the DWFM queues, reduced processing times, and allowed droplet leases to be established.","By 5:28 AM, DWFM had established leases with all droplets within the N.","We will improve the throttling mechanism in our EC2 data propagation systems to rate limit incoming work based on the size of the waiting queue to protect the service during periods of high load.","For EC2, we are building an additional test suite to augment our existing scale testing, which will exercise the DWFM recovery workflow to identify any future regressions.","Starting at 11:48 PM PDT on October 19, these DWFM state checks began to fail as the process depends on DynamoDB and was unable to complete."]},{"id":"aws-2025-dns","org":"AWS","type":"outage","title":"DynamoDB's address went blank: two automated writers raced","blocks":["repl","deploy"],"flagship":false,"date":"2025-10-19","year":2025,"dateText":"19 Oct 2025","system":"DynamoDB is AWS's large key-value database. Its address in us-east-1 (dynamodb.us-east-1.amazonaws.com) is kept up to date by automation: a Planner writes DNS plans (which IP addresses to hand out) and several Enactors apply them.","happened":"One Enactor was running very slowly. Before applying its old plan it had checked that the plan was newer than what was live, but by the time it applied it, that check was stale: a faster Enactor had already applied a newer plan. The slow one overwrote it, and a cleanup step then deleted the old plan, leaving the address with no IP addresses at all. Everything in the region that called DynamoDB failed to find it.","fix":"AWS disabled the DNS Planner and Enactor automation worldwide and will fix the race and add protections against applying incorrect plans before turning it back on.","lesson":"A check made on old information is not a check: two writers need the store itself to reject the older write.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"11:48 PM","t":"start, 19 Oct 2025 PDT","q":3},{"v":"2:40 AM","t":"DynamoDB errors end","q":4},{"v":"2:20 PM","t":"last services recovered, 20 Oct","q":3},{"v":"14 h 32 min","t":"whole event","f":"2:20 PM 20 Oct minus 11:48 PM 19 Oct"}],"terms":[["DNS","the internet's phone book: it turns a name like dynamodb.us-east-1.amazonaws.com into IP addresses"],["race condition","a bug that appears only when two things happen in an unlucky order"],["stale","true when it was read, no longer true when it is used"]],"source":"AWS, Summary of the Amazon DynamoDB Service Disruption in the Northern Virginia (US-EAST-1) Region","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/message/101925/","url2":null,"published":"2025-10","publishedText":"Oct 2025","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["The root cause of this issue was a latent race condition in the DynamoDB DNS management system that resulted in an incorrect empty DNS record for the service's regional endpoint ( dynamodb.us-east-1.amazonaws.com ) that the automation failed to repair.","The race condition involves an unlikely interaction between two of the DNS Enactors.","The check that was made at the start of the plan application process, which ensures that the plan is newer than the previously applied plan, was stale by this time due to the unusually high delays in Enactor processing.","While the event started at 11:48 PM PDT on October 19 and ended at 2:20 PM PDT on October 20, there were three distinct periods of impact to customer applications.","First, between 11:48 PM on October 19 and 2:40 AM on October 20, Amazon DynamoDB experienced increased API error rates in the N.","We have already disabled the DynamoDB DNS Planner and the DNS Enactor automation worldwide.","In advance of re-enabling this automation, we will fix the race condition scenario and add additional protections to prevent the application of incorrect DNS plans."]},{"id":"cf-2025-09","org":"Cloudflare","type":"outage","title":"The dashboard attacked its own API","blocks":["retry","deploy"],"flagship":false,"date":"2025-09-12","year":2025,"dateText":"12 Sep 2025","system":"Cloudflare's dashboard is a web app; when it loads it calls an internal Tenant Service (a Go service on Kubernetes) to fetch which organisations the user belongs to.","happened":"A React bug made the page re-run its API call every time it re-rendered, so every open dashboard sent a stream of repeated requests, retries included. A Tenant Service update went out at the same time; together they overwhelmed the service, which then could not recover. Engineers could not even tell retries from new requests in their metrics.","fix":"A global rate limit on the Tenant Service, much more capacity for it, random delays added to the dashboard's retries, and requests tagged as retry or new. Automatic rollback of the service update would have shortened the second outage.","lesson":"Your own clients can be your biggest attacker: rate-limit internal APIs and add jitter to client retries.","note":null,"numbers":[],"terms":[["rate limit","a cap on how many requests a caller may send per second; extra requests are refused quickly"],["jitter","a random delay added before each retry so many clients do not retry at the same instant"]],"source":"Cloudflare blog, A deep dive into Cloudflare's September 12, 2025 dashboard and API outage (Tom Lianza, Joaquin Madruga)","url":"https://blog.cloudflare.com/deep-dive-into-cloudflares-sept-12-dashboard-and-api-outage/","url2":null,"published":"2025-09-13","publishedText":"13 Sep 2025","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["The API calls were managed by a React useEffect hook, but we mistakenly included a problematic object in its dependency array.","Because this object was recreated on every state or prop change, React treated it as “always new,” causing the useEffect to re-run each time.","This bug caused repeated, unnecessary calls to the Tenant Service API.","This behavior coincided with a service update to the Tenant Service API, compounding instability and ultimately overwhelming the service, which then failed to recover.","We immediately saw an increase in our API usage but found it difficult to identify which requests were retries vs new requests.","We installed a global rate limit on the Tenant Service to help regulate the load.","We'll be introducing changes to the dashboard that include random delays to spread out retries and reduce contention as well.","Had it been in place, we would have automatically rolled back the second Tenant Service update limiting the second outage."]},{"id":"anth-2025-09","org":"Anthropic","type":"outage","title":"Three serving bugs quietly made Claude's answers worse","blocks":["gpu","lb","obs"],"flagship":false,"date":"2025-08-05","year":2025,"dateText":"5 Aug 2025","system":"Claude is served on three hardware platforms (AWS Trainium, NVIDIA GPUs, Google TPUs). A load balancer routes each request to a server pool; some pools were configured for the upcoming 1M-token context window. Routing is sticky: a user's follow-up requests tend to go to the same server.","happened":"From 5 August some requests were misrouted to the 1M-context servers, which degraded answers. A routine load-balancing change on 29 August made it much worse. Two other bugs (a TPU misconfiguration that corrupted output, and a compiler bug in token selection) overlapped. Nothing went down, so error rates looked fine; users noticed before the evals did, and sticky routing meant the unlucky users kept getting the bad servers.","fix":"Fixed the routing logic and rolled back or fixed the other two bugs; Anthropic is adding more sensitive evaluations and running quality evaluations continuously on real production systems.","lesson":"For a model service, wrong answers are an outage that no 5xx counter sees: monitor quality, not only errors.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"16%","t":"of Sonnet 4 requests affected at the worst hour (31 Aug)","q":4},{"v":"30%","t":"of Claude Code users with at least one misrouted message","q":6}],"terms":[["sticky routing","sending a user's requests to the same server each time, usually to reuse its cache"],["context window","how much text (in tokens) a model can read in one request"]],"source":"Anthropic Engineering, A postmortem of three recent issues","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/a-postmortem-of-three-recent-issues","url2":null,"published":"2025-09-17","publishedText":"17 Sep 2025","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Between August and early September, three infrastructure bugs intermittently degraded Claude's response quality.","We deploy Claude across multiple hardware platforms, namely AWS Trainium, NVIDIA GPUs, and Google TPUs.","On August 5, some Sonnet 4 requests were misrouted to servers configured for the upcoming 1M token context window .","On August 29, a routine load balancing change unintentionally increased the number of short-context requests routed to the 1M context servers.","At the worst impacted hour on August 31, 16% of Sonnet 4 requests were affected.","However, some users were affected more severely, as our routing is \"sticky\".","Approximately 30% of Claude Code users who made requests during this period had at least one message routed to the wrong server type, resulting in degraded responses.","More fundamentally, we relied too heavily on noisy evaluations.","Quality evaluations in more places: Although we run regular evaluations on our systems, we will run them continuously on true production systems to catch issues such as the context window load balancing error.","Resolution: We fixed the routing logic to ensure short- and long-context requests were directed to the correct server pools."]},{"id":"gcp-2025-06","org":"Google Cloud","type":"outage","title":"Every region crashed at once, then the restart stampeded the database","blocks":["retry","deploy"],"flagship":false,"date":"2025-06-12","year":2025,"dateText":"12 Jun 2025","system":"Service Control is the Google Cloud component that checks every API request against quotas and policies before it is served. It reads policy data that is replicated worldwide within seconds.","happened":"New quota-check code shipped with no error handling and no feature flag. A policy change with blank fields reached every region, hit the new code path, and crashed Service Control everywhere in a loop. Engineers found it in 10 minutes and flipped the emergency off switch. But in the biggest region, us-central1, all the restarting tasks hit the same database at the same moment (a herd effect), because they had no randomized exponential backoff.","fix":"Throttled how fast tasks were recreated and moved load to other databases; recovery in us-central1 took about 2 h 40 min. Google will make all changes to critical binaries feature-flag protected and off by default, and audit that its systems use randomized exponential backoff.","lesson":"Recovery is a load event: restarts need the same backoff and jitter as retries.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"10 minutes","t":"to identify the root cause","q":2},{"v":"~2h 40 mins","t":"to fully resolve us-central1","q":5}],"terms":[["feature flag","a switch in config that turns new code on gradually (one project, one region) and off instantly"],["exponential backoff","wait 1 s, then 2 s, then 4 s ... before each retry, so retries slow down as failures continue"],["herd effect","many clients doing the same thing at the same instant, so a resource that copes with the average is flattened by the peak"]],"source":"Google Cloud Service Health, incident report: Multiple GCP products are experiencing Service issues (12 June 2025)","url":"https://status.cloud.google.com/incidents/ow5i3PPK96RduMcb1SsW","url2":null,"published":"2025-06","publishedText":"Jun 2025","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["The issue with this change was that it did not have appropriate error handling nor was it feature flag protected.","This pulled in blank fields for this respective policy change and exercised the code path that hit the null pointer causing the binaries to go into a crash loop.","Within 10 minutes, the root cause was identified and the red-button (to disable the serving path) was being put in place.","Within some of our larger regions, such as us-central-1, as Service Control tasks restarted, it created a herd effect on the underlying infrastructure it depends on (i.e.","Service Control did not have the appropriate randomized exponential backoff implemented to avoid this.","It took up to ~2h 40 mins to fully resolve in us-central-1 as we throttled task creation to minimize the impact on the underlying infrastructure and routed traffic to multi-regional databases to reduce the load.","We will audit and ensure our systems employ randomized exponential backoff.","We will enforce all changes to critical binaries to be feature flag protected and disabled by default.","Given the global nature of quota management, this metadata was replicated globally within seconds.","(All Times US/Pacific)  Incident Start: 12 June, 2025 10:49  All regions except us-central1 mitigated: 12 June, 2025 12:48  Incident End: 12 June, 2025 13:49  Duration: 3 hours"]},{"id":"oai-2024-12","org":"OpenAI","type":"outage","title":"A monitoring tool overloaded the cluster brains, and DNS went with them","blocks":["deploy","obs","cache"],"flagship":false,"date":"2024-12-11","year":2024,"dateText":"11 Dec 2024","system":"OpenAI runs its services on Kubernetes clusters. Each cluster has a control plane (the API servers that track what runs where); services find each other through DNS that depends on that control plane.","happened":"A new telemetry service was deployed to every node. On large clusters each node made expensive Kubernetes API calls whose cost grew with cluster size, and the control planes fell over. Running pods kept going, but DNS-based service discovery broke. Per-node DNS caches hid the failure for a while, so the rollout kept going fleet-wide. Engineers then could not reach the control plane to remove the change (the locked-out effect).","fix":"Recovered by scaling down clusters, blocking access to the Kubernetes API and scaling up its servers. Follow-ups: phased rollouts with control-plane health checks for all infrastructure changes, and break-glass access to the API servers in any circumstances.","lesson":"Test changes at the largest scale you run, and keep a way in that does not depend on the thing that broke.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"3:16 PM","t":"start (PST, 11 Dec 2024)","q":4},{"v":"7:38 PM","t":"end","q":4},{"v":"4 h 22 min","t":"of significant degradation or unavailability","f":"7:38 PM minus 3:16 PM"}],"terms":[["Kubernetes","software that decides which machine runs each copy of each service and restarts them when they die"],["control plane","the part of a system that manages and configures it, as opposed to the data plane that serves the requests"],["break-glass","an emergency access path kept separate from normal tooling"]],"source":"OpenAI status page, API, ChatGPT & Sora Facing Issues (write-up of December 11, 2024)","url":"https://status.openai.com/incidents/ctrsv3lwd797","url2":null,"published":"2024-12-13","publishedText":"13 Dec 2024","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["At 3:12 PM PST, we deployed a new telemetry service to collect detailed Kubernetes control plane metrics.","Telemetry services have a very wide footprint, so this new service's configuration unintentionally caused every node in each cluster to execute resource-intensive Kubernetes API operations whose cost scaled with the size of the cluster.","The Kubernetes data plane can operate largely independently of the control plane, but DNS relies on the control plane – services don't know how to contact one another without the Kubernetes control plane.","The impact was specific to clusters exceeding a certain size, and our DNS cache on each node delayed visible failures long enough for the rollout to continue.","Impact Between 3:16 PM PST and 7:38 PM PST on December 11, 2024, all OpenAI services experienced significant degradation or complete unavailability.","Remediation was very slow because of the locked out effect.","We'll implement break-glass mechanisms to ensure that engineers can access Kubernetes API servers in any circumstances.","We identified the issue within minutes and immediately spun up multiple workstreams to explore different ways to bring our clusters back online quickly: Scaling down cluster size: Reduced the aggregate Kubernetes API load.","Blocking network access to Kubernetes admin APIs: Prevented new expensive requests, giving the API servers time to recover.","Scaling up Kubernetes API servers: Increased available resources to handle pending requests, allowing us to apply the fix."]},{"id":"crowd-2024-07","org":"CrowdStrike","type":"outage","title":"One content update, pushed to everyone at once, crashed 8.5 million PCs","blocks":["deploy"],"flagship":false,"date":"2024-07-19","year":2024,"dateText":"19 Jul 2024","system":"CrowdStrike's Falcon sensor runs inside the Windows kernel to detect attacks. Besides software releases, it receives frequent content updates (channel files) that tell it what to look for.","happened":"A new template type expected 21 input fields; the sensor code supplied 20. The validator checked the content against the 21-field definition and passed it. When a content update used the 21st field, the sensor read past the end of its array, and because it runs in the kernel, Windows crashed. The update went to every online machine at once: content updates were not rolled out in stages.","fix":"Remediated the content 78 minutes after release. CrowdStrike added bounds checks, a validator check for the field count, and staged deployment for content updates.","lesson":"Content and config can crash code like any release: stage them, and the more privileged the code, the smaller the first stage.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"04:09 UTC","t":"update released (19 Jul 2024)","q":0},{"v":"05:27 UTC","t":"update remediated","q":1},{"v":"78 min","t":"exposure window","f":"05:27 minus 04:09"},{"v":"21","t":"fields the template defined","q":2},{"v":"20","t":"fields the sensor supplied","q":2},{"v":"8.5 million","t":"Windows devices affected (Microsoft's estimate)","q":5}],"terms":[["kernel","the core of the operating system; a crash there takes the whole machine down"],["out-of-bounds read","reading memory past the end of an array, which can crash the program"]],"source":"CrowdStrike, Channel File 291 Incident Root Cause Analysis (PDF, 6 August 2024); CrowdStrike technical details post; Microsoft blog (20 July 2024)","url":"https://www.crowdstrike.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Channel-File-291-Incident-Root-Cause-Analysis-08.06.2024.pdf","url2":"https://blogs.microsoft.com/blog/2024/07/20/helping-our-customers-through-the-crowdstrike-outage/","published":"2024-08-06","publishedText":"6 Aug 2024","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["On July 19, 2024 at 04:09 UTC, as part of ongoing operations, CrowdStrike released a sensor configuration update to Windows systems.","The sensor configuration update that caused the system crash was remediated on Friday, July 19, 2024 05:27 UTC.","The new IPC Template Type defined 21 input parameter fields, but the integration code that invoked the Content Interpreter with Channel File 291's Template Instances supplied only 20 input values to match against.","As a result, once Rapid Response Content was delivered that used a non-wildcard matching criterion for the 21st input, the Content Interpreter performed an out-of-bounds read of the input array.","Template Instances should have staged deployment Findings: Each Template Instance should be deployed in a staged rollout.","We currently estimate that CrowdStrike's update affected 8.5 million Windows devices, or less than one percent of all Windows machines."]},{"id":"cai-2024","org":"Character.AI","type":"architecture","title":"20,000 LLM queries a second: shrink the KV cache, then keep it between turns","blocks":["gpu","cache","lb"],"flagship":false,"date":"2024-06-20","year":2024,"dateText":"20 Jun 2024","system":"Character.AI serves chat with its own LLMs. During generation a model keeps a KV cache: intermediate results for every earlier token of the conversation, held in GPU memory.","happened":"The KV cache, not the GPU's arithmetic, limited how many chats fit on a GPU. Character.AI shrank it more than 20 times with architecture choices (multi-query attention, sharing the cache across layers, int8). It also keeps each conversation's KV cache in host memory between turns, and uses sticky sessions so the next message of a dialogue reaches the server that holds its cache.","fix":"A 95% cache hit rate, and serving costs 33 times lower than at the end of 2022.","lesson":"For LLM serving, memory per conversation is capacity; caching across turns needs routing that keeps a conversation on its server.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"20,000","t":"inference queries per second","q":0},{"v":"20X","t":"smaller KV cache","q":1},{"v":"95%","t":"cache rate","q":4},{"v":"33","t":"times lower serving cost than late 2022","q":5}],"terms":[["KV cache","the stored keys and values of earlier tokens, reused so the model does not recompute the whole conversation for each new token"],["sticky session","routing all requests of one conversation to the same server"]],"source":"Character.AI research blog, Optimizing AI Inference at Character.AI (read from the Wayback Machine)","url":"https://research.character.ai/optimizing-inference/","url2":null,"published":"2024-06-20","publishedText":"20 Jun 2024","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Today we serve more than 20,000 inference queries per second.","We use the following techniques to reduce KV cache size by more than 20X without regressing quality.","One of our key innovations is an efficient system for caching attention KV on host memory between chat turns.","At a fleet level, we use sticky sessions to route the queries from the same dialogue to the same server.","Our system achieves a 95% cache rate, further reducing inference cost.","We have reduced serving costs by a factor of 33 compared to when we began in late 2022.","Today, if we were to serve our traffic using leading commercial APIs, it would cost at least 13.5X more than with our systems.","The key bottleneck of LLM inference throughput is the size of the cache of attention keys and values (KV)."]},{"id":"figma-2024","org":"Figma","type":"architecture","title":"Sharding Postgres without leaving Postgres","blocks":["shard"],"flagship":false,"date":"2024-03-14","year":2024,"dateText":"14 Mar 2024","system":"Figma runs on Amazon RDS PostgreSQL. It first split by table (vertical partitioning: groups of tables on separate databases).","happened":"Some tables reached several terabytes and billions of rows and were heading past the I/O limits (IOPS) of the largest RDS instance. Figma built horizontal sharding itself: a DBProxy service parses each SQL query and routes it to the right shard, related tables share a shard key (\"colos\"), and logical sharding was rolled out before physical sharding so that it could be tested and rolled back.","fix":"First horizontally sharded table shipped in September 2023.","lesson":"Vertical partitioning buys time; for the biggest tables only horizontal sharding scales, and a routing layer lets you add it gradually.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"100x","t":"growth of the database stack since 2020","q":0}],"terms":[["vertical partitioning","moving whole tables to different databases"],["horizontal sharding","splitting the rows of one table across databases"],["IOPS","disk reads and writes per second, a hard limit of each database machine"]],"source":"Figma blog, How Figma's databases team lived to tell the scale (Sammy Steele)","url":"https://www.figma.com/blog/how-figmas-databases-team-lived-to-tell-the-scale/","url2":null,"published":"2024-03-14","publishedText":"14 Mar 2024","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Figma's database stack has grown almost 100x since 2020.","Our highest write tables were growing so quickly that we would soon exceed the maximum IO operations per second (IOPS) supported by Amazon's Relational Database Service (RDS).","Despite our incremental scaling progress, we always knew that vertical partitioning could only get us so far.","DBProxy query engine: We built a DBProxy service that intercepts SQL queries generated by our application layer, and dynamically routes queries to various Postgres databases.","We shipped our first horizontally sharded table in September 2023.","The data revealed that some of our tables, containing several terabytes and billions of rows, were becoming too large for a single database.","We leveraged views to perform a safer and lower cost logical sharding rollout before we executed a riskier distributed physical failover."]},{"id":"anyscale-2023","org":"Anyscale","type":"architecture","title":"Continuous batching: let a finished answer leave the batch at once","blocks":["gpu","queue"],"flagship":false,"date":"2023-06-22","year":2023,"dateText":"22 Jun 2023","system":"An LLM server batches requests so that one pass over the model's weights serves several answers at once. Answers have different lengths.","happened":"With static batching the server waits until the longest answer in the batch is finished; the slots of answers that ended early sit idle, and new requests queue outside. Continuous batching (iteration-level scheduling, from the Orca paper) re-forms the batch after every generated token: a finished answer leaves and a waiting request joins at once. The benchmark ran on one NVIDIA A100.","fix":"8x throughput over naive static batching from continuous batching alone, and 23x with vLLM's memory management on top, while median latency also fell (the authors' benchmark).","lesson":"A batch should be a queue that keeps moving, not a bus that leaves only when full and returns only when empty.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"8x","t":"throughput from continuous batching over naive static batching","q":3},{"v":"23x","t":"with vLLM","q":4}],"terms":[["static batching","the batch is fixed until every request in it has finished"],["p50","the median latency: half the requests are faster"],["iteration","one step of generation, producing one token for every answer in the batch"]],"source":"Anyscale blog, How continuous batching enables 23x throughput in LLM inference while reducing p50 latency (Cade Daniel, Chen Shen, Eric Liang, Richard Liaw)","url":"https://www.anyscale.com/blog/continuous-batching-llm-inference","url2":null,"published":"2023-06-22","publishedText":"22 Jun 2023","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Even though sequence 3 finished after two iterations, static batching means that the GPU will be underutilized until the last sequence in the batch finishes generation (in this example, sequence 2 after six iterations).","Instead of waiting until every sequence in a batch has completed generation, Orca implements iteration-level scheduling where the batch size is determined per iteration.","Link Hardware/model We benchmark throughput and latency on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU provided by Anyscale .","8x throughput over naive batching by using continuous batching (both on Ray Serve and Hugging Face’s text-generation-inference ).","By leveraging vLLM, users can achieve 23x LLM inference throughput while reducing p50 latency.","Continuous batching frameworks currently manage this via hyperparameter: waiting_served_ratio , or the ratio of requests waiting for prefill to those waiting end-of-sequence tokens."]},{"id":"vllm-2023","org":"vLLM / LMSYS","type":"architecture","title":"PagedAttention: GPU memory managed like an operating system's","blocks":["gpu"],"flagship":false,"date":"2023-06-20","year":2023,"dateText":"20 Jun 2023","system":"vLLM, an open-source LLM serving engine from UC Berkeley, used to serve LMSYS's Chatbot Arena.","happened":"Earlier servers reserved one contiguous block of GPU memory per request, sized for the longest possible answer, wasting 60 to 80% of KV-cache memory. PagedAttention stores the cache in small fixed-size pages allocated as the answer grows, like virtual memory in an operating system, so waste falls under 4% and many more requests fit in one batch.","fix":"Up to 24x the throughput of Hugging Face Transformers in the authors' tests; LMSYS halved the GPUs needed for its traffic.","lesson":"When GPU memory is the limit, wasted memory is lost throughput.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"60% – 80%","t":"memory wasted by earlier systems","q":0},{"v":"under 4%","t":"waste with PagedAttention","q":1},{"v":"24x","t":"throughput against HF Transformers (authors' benchmark)","q":2},{"v":"50%","t":"fewer GPUs for LMSYS's traffic","q":3}],"terms":[["throughput","how many requests (or tokens) are finished per second"],["batch","several requests run through the model together, sharing each pass over the weights"]],"source":"vLLM blog, vLLM: Easy, Fast, and Cheap LLM Serving with PagedAttention (Kwon, Li, Zhuang et al.)","url":"https://blog.vllm.ai/2023/06/20/vllm.html","url2":null,"published":"2023-06-20","publishedText":"20 Jun 2023","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["We find that existing systems waste 60% – 80% of memory due to fragmentation and over-reservation.","In practice, this results in near-optimal memory usage, with a mere waste of under 4%.","In our experiments, vLLM achieves up to 24x higher throughput compared to HF and up to 3.5x higher throughput than TGI.","With vLLM, LMSYS was able to cut the number of GPUs used for serving the above traffic by 50%.","vLLM has been developed at UC Berkeley and deployed at Chatbot Arena and Vicuna Demo for the past two months.","To address this problem, we introduce PagedAttention , an attention algorithm inspired by the classic idea of virtual memory and paging in operating systems."]},{"id":"discord-2023","org":"Discord","type":"architecture","title":"Trillions of messages: hot partitions, request coalescing, a new database","blocks":["shard","cache","repl"],"flagship":false,"date":"2023-03-06","year":2023,"dateText":"6 Mar 2023","system":"Discord stores every message in a distributed database partitioned by channel and time bucket; at the start of 2022 it was a 177-node Cassandra cluster.","happened":"A busy channel's partition becomes hot: thousands of users read the same rows at once. Because reads and writes needed a quorum of replicas, slow nodes for that partition slowed everyone. Discord put data services (written in Rust) between the API and the database: concurrent requests for the same data are coalesced into one database query, and consistent hashing routes all requests for a channel to the same data-service instance so coalescing works. Then it migrated to ScyllaDB, a Cassandra-compatible database without a garbage collector.","fix":"177 Cassandra nodes became 72 ScyllaDB nodes; the migration ran at up to 3.2 million messages per second and took nine days.","lesson":"Before buying a bigger database, stop sending it the same query a thousand times.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"177","t":"Cassandra nodes before","q":0},{"v":"72","t":"ScyllaDB nodes after","q":5},{"v":"15ms","t":"p99 to fetch history after (40-125ms before)","q":8},{"v":"3.2 million","t":"messages per second during the migration","q":6}],"terms":[["partition","the slice of data stored together on the same replicas, chosen by a key"],["quorum","a majority of replicas; a read or write waits for that many to answer"],["request coalescing","when many callers ask for the same thing at once, make one backend request and share the answer"],["consistent hashing","a way to map keys to servers so that adding a server moves only a small share of keys"]],"source":"Discord blog, How Discord Stores Trillions of Messages (Bo Ingram)","url":"https://discord.com/blog/how-discord-stores-trillions-of-messages","url2":null,"published":"2023-03-06","publishedText":"6 Mar 2023","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["At the beginning of 2022, it had 177 nodes with trillions of messages.","Lots of concurrent reads as users interact with servers can hotspot a partition, which we refer to imaginatively as a “hot partition”.","Since we perform reads and writes with quorum consistency level, all queries to the nodes that serve the hot partition suffer latency increases, resulting in broader end-user impact.","The big feature our data services provide is request coalescing.","We implemented consistent hash-based routing to our data services to enable more effective coalescing.","It's a much more efficient database -- we're going from running 177 Cassandra nodes to just 72 ScyllaDB nodes.","We turn it on and leave it running, migrating messages at speeds of up to 3.2 million per second.","We hook up our new and improved migrator and get a new estimate: nine days!","For example, fetching historical messages had a p99 of between 40-125ms on Cassandra, with ScyllaDB having a nice and chill 15ms p99 latency, and message insert performance going from 5-70ms p99 on Cassandra, to a steady 5ms p99 on ScyllaDB.","We partition our messages by the channel they’re sent in, along with a bucket, which is a static time window.","Although ScyllaDB is most definitely not void of issues, it is void of a garbage collector, since it’s written in C++ rather than Java.","Our data services sit between the API and our ScyllaDB clusters."]},{"id":"roblox-2021-10","org":"Roblox","type":"outage","title":"73 hours down: the service directory everything depends on choked","blocks":["repl","obs"],"flagship":false,"date":"2021-10-28","year":2021,"dateText":"28 Oct 2021","system":"Roblox runs hundreds of internal services on its own servers. They find each other through HashiCorp Consul, a small replicated cluster that holds the service directory and settings and agrees on writes through leader election (Raft).","happened":"Roblox had enabled a new Consul streaming feature and added 50% more routing nodes ahead of year-end traffic. Under unusually high read and write load, streaming caused heavy contention, and a pathological performance problem in Consul's storage library (BoltDB) made writes slow. Consul degraded, so almost every service degraded. Roblox's own monitoring depended on the same systems, which slowed diagnosis.","fix":"Turned off streaming, reset the Consul cluster from a snapshot, and let players back in by percentage, checking database and cache load at each step. Telemetry no longer depends on the systems it monitors.","lesson":"Find your one shared dependency and keep your monitoring outside it.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"73-hour","t":"outage, 28 to 31 Oct 2021","q":0},{"v":"50%","t":"more traffic-routing nodes added before the incident","q":1}],"terms":[["service discovery","how a service finds the current addresses of the services it calls"],["leader election","replicas choosing one of themselves to accept writes, so they agree on order"]],"source":"Roblox, Roblox Return to Service 10/28-10/31 2021","url":"https://about.roblox.com/newsroom/2022/01/roblox-return-to-service-10-28-10-31-2021","url2":null,"published":"2022-01-20","publishedText":"20 Jan 2022","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Starting October 28th and fully resolving on October 31st, Roblox experienced a 73-hour outage.","As part of this rollout, in order to prepare for the increased traffic we typically see at the end of the year, we also increased the number of nodes supporting traffic routing by 50%.","Enabling a relatively new streaming feature on Consul under unusually high read and write load led to excessive contention and poor performance.","In addition, our particular load conditions triggered a pathological performance issue in BoltDB.","By turning off streaming, we dramatically improved the health of the Consul cluster.","We have removed this circular dependency. Our telemetry systems no longer depend on the systems that they are configured to monitor.","Every time we increased the percentage, we checked database load, cache performance, and overall system stability.","The team decided to shut down the entire Consul cluster and reset its state using a snapshot from a few hours before – the beginning of the outage."]},{"id":"notion-2021","org":"Notion","type":"architecture","title":"Why 480 shards: a Postgres monolith split by workspace","blocks":["shard","repl"],"flagship":false,"date":"2021-10-06","year":2021,"dateText":"6 Oct 2021","system":"Notion's blocks (every paragraph, page and to-do) lived in one PostgreSQL database that had grown through four orders of magnitude.","happened":"On-call engineers were woken by CPU spikes and even simple schema changes became risky. Notion partitioned by workspace ID, since every block belongs to exactly one workspace, into 480 logical shards on 32 physical databases (15 each). 480 has many divisors, so it can later spread evenly over 40 or 48 hosts. The move used double-writing (new writes go to both), a backfill of old data, verification, then a switch-over.","fix":"Sharded Postgres with application-level routing.","lesson":"Pick the shard key that keeps each request on one shard (here, the workspace), and a shard count that divides many ways.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"480","t":"logical shards","q":3},{"v":"32","t":"physical databases","q":3},{"v":"15","t":"logical shards per database","f":"480 / 32"},{"v":"32 to 40 to 48","t":"future host counts that keep shards even","q":4}],"terms":[["partition key","the field that decides which shard a row goes to"],["double-write","during a migration, write every change to both the old and the new store"]],"source":"Notion blog, Herding elephants: Lessons learned from sharding Postgres at Notion","url":"https://www.notion.com/blog/sharding-postgres-at-notion","url2":null,"published":"2021-10-06","publishedText":"6 Oct 2021","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["By mid-2020, it was clear that product usage would surpass the abilities of our trusty Postgres monolith, which had served us dutifully through five years and four orders of magnitude of growth.","Engineers on-call often woke up to database CPU spikes, and simple catalog-only migrations became unsafe and uncertain.","Because each row in a sharded table is either a block or related to one, and each block belongs to exactly one workspace , we used the workspace ID as the partition key .","After crunching the numbers, we settled on an architecture consisting of 480 logical shards evenly distributed across 32 physical databases .","For example, in the future we could scale from 32 to 40 to 48 hosts, making incremental jumps each time.","Backfill: Once double-writing has begun, migrate the old data to the new database.","Verification: Ensure the integrity of data in the new database."]},{"id":"fb-2021-10","org":"Meta","type":"outage","title":"One maintenance command disconnected every Facebook data center","blocks":["deploy","lb"],"flagship":false,"date":"2021-10-04","year":2021,"dateText":"4 Oct 2021","system":"Facebook's data centers are joined by its own backbone network. Its DNS servers announce themselves to the internet with BGP, and withdraw those announcements if they cannot reach the data centers, a safety rule meant to steer users away from a broken site.","happened":"A command meant to measure backbone capacity took down all backbone links; a bug in the audit tool let it through. With the backbone gone, every DNS server judged itself unhealthy and withdrew from the internet, so facebook.com stopped resolving at all. Internal tools broke with DNS and remote access was down, so engineers were sent onsite.","fix":"Restored the backbone on site, then brought traffic back gradually, because switching tens of megawatts of load back on at once could itself break power systems and caches.","lesson":"A health rule that is correct for one site can take down everything when every site trips it at the same time.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"tens of megawatts","t":"power dip per data center to be restored carefully","q":3}],"terms":[["backbone","the private long-distance network linking a company's data centers"],["BGP","the protocol networks use to tell the rest of the internet \"send traffic for these addresses to me\""]],"source":"Engineering at Meta, More details about the October 4 outage (Santosh Janardhan)","url":"https://engineering.fb.com/2021/10/05/networking-traffic/outage-details/","url2":null,"published":"2021-10-05","publishedText":"5 Oct 2021","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["During one of these routine maintenance jobs, a command was issued with the intention to assess the availability of global backbone capacity, which unintentionally took down all the connections in our backbone network, effectively disconnecting Facebook data centers globally.","Our systems are designed to audit commands like these to prevent mistakes like this, but a bug in that audit tool prevented it from properly stopping the command.","To ensure reliable operation, our DNS servers disable those BGP advertisements if they themselves can not speak to our data centers, since this is an indication of an unhealthy network connection.","Individual data centers were reporting dips in power usage in the range of tens of megawatts, and suddenly reversing such a dip in power consumption could put everything from electrical systems to caches at risk.","Our primary and out-of-band network access was down, so we sent engineers onsite to the data centers to have them debug the issue and restart the systems.","The end result was that our DNS servers became unreachable even though they were still operational."]},{"id":"slack-2021-01","org":"Slack","type":"outage","title":"The first Monday of the year: autoscaling made it worse","blocks":["lb","obs"],"flagship":false,"date":"2021-01-04","year":2021,"dateText":"4 Jan 2021","system":"Slack's servers live in many AWS virtual networks joined by AWS Transit Gateways. An autoscaler adds or removes web servers by load, and a provision-service sets up each new server.","happened":"Traffic on the first working day of 2021 overloaded a Transit Gateway, which dropped packets. Web servers waiting on the network used less CPU, so the autoscaler read them as idle and removed some; then, as worker threads piled up, it tried to add 1,200 servers in 14 minutes. The provisioning service hit the Linux open-files limit and an AWS quota, so new servers did not come up. Slack's dashboards ran in another network and were blind for the same reason.","fix":"AWS raised the gateway's capacity by hand; Slack fixed provision-service limits, will load test it, revisit health checks and autoscaling, and ask for pre-emptive gateway scaling after holidays.","lesson":"Autoscaling reads a signal; when the network is the bottleneck, the signal lies. Load-test the scaling path itself.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"1,200","t":"servers the autoscaler tried to add","q":2},{"v":"14 min","t":"window for that scale-up","f":"7:15 minus 7:01 AM PST"}],"terms":[["autoscaling","software that adds servers when a load metric goes up and removes them when it goes down"],["quota","a cap the cloud provider puts on how many of a resource you may use"]],"source":"Engineering at Slack, Slack's Outage on January 4th 2021","url":"https://slack.engineering/slacks-outage-on-january-4th-2021/","url2":null,"published":"2021-02-01","publishedText":"1 Feb 2021","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["On January 4th, one of our Transit Gateways became overloaded.","Secondly, our autoscaling system downscaled our web tier.","We attempted to add 1,200 servers to our web tier between 7:01am PST and 7:15am PST.","The spike of load from the simultaneous provisioning of so many instances under suboptimal network conditions meant that provision-service hit two separate resource bottlenecks (the most significant one was the Linux open files limit, but we also exceeded an AWS quota limit).","In this case, our dashboard and alerting services failed because they were running in a different VPC from their backend databases, creating a dependency on the TGWs.","We've also set ourselves a reminder (a Slack reminder , of course) to request a preemptive upscaling of our TGWs at the end of the next holiday season.","The network problems prior to 7:00am PST meant that the threads were spending more time waiting, which caused CPU utilization to drop.","This drop in CPU utilization initially triggered some automated downscaling."]},{"id":"abl-timeouts","org":"AWS","type":"architecture","title":"Five layers that each retry three times hit the database 243 times","blocks":["retry","degrade"],"flagship":false,"date":"2020-02-27","year":2020,"dateText":"27 Feb 2020","system":"Amazon's internal guidance (the Builders' Library) on timeouts, retries and backoff, by Marc Brooker.","happened":"Retries multiply across layers. If a request passes through five services and each makes up to three attempts, one failing database call can turn into 3 x 3 x 3 x 3 x 3 = 243 calls, exactly when the database is struggling. Amazon's practices: set a timeout on every remote call (chosen from the dependency's measured latency, for example its p99.9), retry only at one layer, cap retries with a local token bucket, retry only while the dependency looks healthy, and retry only APIs that are idempotent.","fix":"Published as Amazon's standing practice for its own services.","lesson":"Retries are load: budget them, and retry at one layer, not at every layer.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"243x","t":"load on the database with five layers each retrying","q":2},{"v":"3^5 = 243","t":"three attempts per layer, five layers","f":"3 x 3 x 3 x 3 x 3"}],"terms":[["timeout","the longest a caller will wait for an answer before giving up"],["p99.9","the latency that 99.9% of requests beat; only 1 in 1,000 is slower"],["token bucket","a counter that refills at a steady rate; each retry spends a token, and when it is empty retries stop"]],"source":"Amazon Builders' Library, Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter (Marc Brooker); read from the Wayback Machine because the live address now redirects","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/","url2":"https://web.archive.org/web/2024/https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/","published":"2019 (first archived capture 2020-02-27)","publishedText":"2019 (first archived capture 2020-02-27)","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Retries can amplify the load on a dependent system.","Consider a system where the customer's call causes a five-deep stack of service calls.","If each layer retries independently, the load on the database will increase 243x, making it unlikely to ever recover.","This is because the retries at each layer multiply -- first three tries, then nine tries, and so on.","We have found that we can mitigate this risk by limiting retries locally using a token bucket .","We avoid this amplification by retrying only when we observe that the dependency is healthy.","In general, our view is that APIs with side effects aren't safe to retry unless they provide idempotency.","Then, we look at the corresponding latency percentile on the downstream service (p99.9 in this example).","In general, for low-cost control-plane and data-plane operations, our best practice is to retry at a single point in the stack."]},{"id":"gh-2018-10","org":"GitHub","type":"outage","title":"43 seconds of network loss, 24 hours of repair","blocks":["repl"],"flagship":false,"date":"2018-10-21","year":2018,"dateText":"21 Oct 2018","system":"GitHub stored its data in MySQL clusters with one primary (the copy that accepts writes) and replicas in its East and West Coast data centers. A tool called Orchestrator promotes a replica to primary automatically when the primary looks dead.","happened":"Network maintenance cut the East Coast data center off for 43 seconds. Orchestrator promoted West Coast replicas to primary. Now both coasts held writes the other did not have, so GitHub could not simply fail back; and East Coast apps writing across the country were too slow. GitHub chose data integrity over a shorter outage: it restored from backups, re-synchronised the replicas and replayed the backlog over a day.","fix":"Orchestrator was set to stop promoting primaries across regions; GitHub sped up an effort already under way to serve from several data centers at once (active/active/active).","lesson":"Automatic failover across a slow link trades a short outage for a long consistency repair.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"43 seconds","t":"network partition","q":1},{"v":"24 hours and 11 minutes","t":"of degraded service","q":1},{"v":"200,000","t":"webhook payloads dropped after outliving their TTL","q":4}],"terms":[["primary and replica","the copy that takes writes, and the copies that receive them a moment later"],["failover","promoting a replica to primary when the primary is judged dead"],["partition","here, a network split: two parts of a system cannot talk to each other"]],"source":"GitHub blog, October 21 post-incident analysis","url":"https://github.blog/news-insights/company-news/oct21-post-incident-analysis/","url2":null,"published":"2018-10-30","publishedText":"30 Oct 2018","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["At 22:52 UTC on October 21, routine maintenance work to replace failing 100G optical equipment resulted in the loss of connectivity between our US East Coast network hub and our primary US East Coast data center.","Connectivity between these locations was restored in 43 seconds, but this brief outage triggered a chain of events that led to 24 hours and 11 minutes of service degradation.","Because the database clusters in both data centers now contained writes that were not present in the other data center, we were unable to fail the primary back over to the US East Coast data center safely.","However, applications running in the East Coast that depend on writing information to a West Coast MySQL cluster are currently unable to cope with the additional latency introduced by a cross-country round trip for the majority of their database calls.","As we re-enabled processing of this data, we processed ~200,000 webhook payloads that had outlived an internal TTL and were dropped.","Adjust the configuration of Orchestrator to prevent the promotion of database primaries across regional boundaries.","In other words, our strategy was to prioritize data integrity over site usability and time to recovery.","In the weeks prior to this incident, we had started a company-wide engineering initiative to support serving GitHub traffic from multiple data centers in an active/active/active design."]},{"id":"shopify-2018","org":"Shopify","type":"architecture","title":"Pods: many small isolated Shopifys behind one front door","blocks":["shard","lb","degrade"],"flagship":false,"date":"2018-03-02","year":2018,"dateText":"2 Mar 2018","system":"Shopify hosts many shops on one platform, and one busy shop must not take database time from all the others.","happened":"Shops are grouped into pods; each pod has its own fully isolated datastores, while app servers, job workers and load balancers are shared. A component in the load balancers called Sorting Hat matches each request to its pod and tags it with a header, so the app connects to that pod's databases. Pods are the unit of failure and of disaster recovery: a whole data center is evacuated pod by pod.","fix":"A pod failing is meant to stay a pod failure, not a platform outage.","lesson":"Shard by tenant and isolate the shards, so one customer's bad day stays theirs.","note":null,"numbers":[],"terms":[["tenant","one customer (here, one shop) sharing a platform with others"],["blast radius","how much breaks when one part fails"]],"source":"Shopify Engineering, A Pods Architecture To Allow Shopify To Scale (read from the Wayback Machine)","url":"https://shopify.engineering/a-pods-architecture-to-allow-shopify-to-scale","url2":null,"published":"2018-03-02","publishedText":"2 Mar 2018","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["A pod consists of a set of shops that live on a fully isolated set of datastores.","Outside of the isolated datastores, pods also have shared resources such as job workers, app servers, and load balancers.","To address this we introduced a piece of software in our load balancers called Sorting Hat.","Using a list of rules Sorting Hat matches every request to a pod and adds a header to that request.","Having disaster recovery working on the level of individual pods became very important to uphold our promise that a single pod's failure wouldn't spiral to a platform outage.","A pod forms a convenient atom for our disaster recovery tooling, evacuating a whole data center is nothing more than evacuating each pod active there one at a time.","Fully isolated, every datastore buys us capacity and prevents a single shop from sucking up database time across the platform but does nothing to solve our problem from above."]},{"id":"stripe-2017","org":"Stripe","type":"architecture","title":"Idempotency keys: retry a payment without charging twice","blocks":["retry"],"flagship":false,"date":"2017-02-22","year":2017,"dateText":"22 Feb 2017","system":"Stripe's API creates charges. A client (a shop's server) sends \"charge this card $20\" over a network that can fail at any point.","happened":"If the connection drops, the client cannot tell whether the charge happened. Retrying risks charging twice; not retrying risks not charging. Stripe's answer: the client generates a unique key (a random UUID) per operation and sends it as an Idempotency-Key header. The server remembers the key and its result, so a retry with the same key returns the first result instead of charging again. Its client library retries automatically, with exponential backoff and jitter so many clients do not retry in step.","fix":"Keys are up to 255 characters; Stripe may remove a key once it is at least 24 hours old, so a retry must come within that window.","lesson":"Make any operation with side effects safe to repeat before you let anyone retry it.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"255","t":"maximum key length (characters)","q":5},{"v":"24 hours","t":"minimum age before a key may be removed","q":6}],"terms":[["idempotent","doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once"],["UUID","a 128-bit random identifier; two clients will practically never pick the same one"]],"source":"Stripe blog, Designing robust and predictable APIs with idempotency (Brandur Leach); Stripe API reference, Idempotent requests","url":"https://stripe.com/blog/idempotency","url2":"https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests","published":"2017-02-22","publishedText":"22 Feb 2017","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["When a client sees that a network operation has failed, there's a good chance that it's due to an intermittent failure that will be gone by the next retry.","This is known as the thundering herd problem .","We can address thundering herd by adding some amount of random “jitter” to each client's wait time.","The Stripe Ruby library retries on failure automatically with an idempotency key using increasing backoff times and jitter.","How you create unique keys is up to you, but we suggest using V4 UUIDs, or another random string with enough entropy to avoid collisions.","Idempotency keys are up to 255 characters long.","You can remove keys from the system automatically after they're at least 24 hours old."]},{"id":"gitlab-2017-01","org":"GitLab","type":"outage","title":"Deleted the wrong database, then found the backups did not work","blocks":["repl","obs"],"flagship":false,"date":"2017-01-31","year":2017,"dateText":"31 Jan 2017","system":"GitLab.com ran one PostgreSQL primary with a replica, plus supposedly several kinds of backup.","happened":"Late in the evening (UTC), under heavy load, the replica fell behind. An engineer trying to reset it ran the delete command on the primary. The regular pg_dump backups had been failing silently (a major-version mismatch), and disk snapshots were not enabled. The best copy left was a snapshot taken by hand 6 hours earlier, on a slow staging disk.","fix":"Restored from that snapshot (copying took about 18 hours), losing about 6 hours of database changes. Follow-ups included a dashboard showing whether backups ran, and owners for testing recovery procedures.","lesson":"A replica is not a backup, and a backup you have not restored is not a backup.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"6 hours","t":"age of the only usable snapshot","q":3},{"v":"18 hours","t":"to copy the data back","q":4},{"v":"5,000","t":"projects affected (and 5,000 comments, 700 new accounts)","q":5}],"terms":[["pg_dump","PostgreSQL's tool that writes a database out as a file"],["snapshot","a frozen copy of a disk at one moment"]],"source":"GitLab blog, Postmortem of database outage of January 31","url":"https://about.gitlab.com/blog/postmortem-of-database-outage-of-january-31/","url2":null,"published":"2017-02-10","publishedText":"10 Feb 2017","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["- The database directory of the primary database was removed by accident, instead of removing the database directory of the secondary.","Database backups using pg_dump When we went to look for the pg_dump backups we found out they were not there.","- Azure disk snapshots were not enabled for the database servers, and the periodic database backups using pg_dump were not working.","To recover GitLab.com we decided to use the LVM snapshot created 6 hours before the outage, as it was our only option to reduce data loss as much as possible (the alternative was to lose almost 24 hours of data).","Copying the data from the staging to the production host took around 18 hours.","Our best estimate is that it affected roughly 5,000 projects, 5,000 comments and 700 new user accounts.","± 23:00 UTC: Due to the increased load, our PostgreSQL secondary's replication process started to lag behind.","A difference in major versions results in pg_dump producing an error, terminating the backup procedure."]},{"id":"sre-2016","org":"Google","type":"architecture","title":"Error budgets: how much failure you are allowed to spend","blocks":["obs","deploy"],"flagship":false,"date":"2016-04-01","year":2016,"dateText":"1 Apr 2016","system":"Google's Site Reliability Engineering book, chapter Embracing Risk.","happened":"Instead of aiming for 100% uptime, a service picks a target (a Service Level Objective). The gap between the target and 100% is the error budget. While budget remains, teams ship; when it is spent, releases slow down. This turns the argument between shipping fast and staying up into arithmetic both sides agree on.","fix":"Releases continue while budget remains; the budget gives product and SRE teams a shared incentive.","lesson":"Pick a reliability target, measure against it, and spend the remainder on change.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"99.99%","t":"example availability target","q":0},{"v":"52.56 minutes","t":"down per year that 99.99% allows","q":0},{"v":"250","t":"errors allowed in a day of 2.5M requests at 99.99%","q":1}],"terms":[["SLO","Service Level Objective: the reliability target, such as 99.9% of requests succeed in 30 days"],["error budget","1 minus the SLO: the failures you can afford"]],"source":"Google, Site Reliability Engineering (O'Reilly, 2016), chapter 3: Embracing Risk","url":"https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/","url2":null,"published":"2016-04","publishedText":"Apr 2016","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["For example, a system with an availability target of 99.99% can be down for up to 52.56 minutes in a year and stay within its availability target; see Availability Table for a table.","For example, a system that serves 2.5M requests in a day with a daily availability target of 99.99% can serve up to 250 errors and still hit its target for that given day.","As long as the uptime measured is above the SLO -- in other words, as long as there is error budget remaining -- new releases can be pushed.","An error budget aligns incentives and emphasizes joint ownership between SRE and product development."]},{"id":"aws-jitter-2015","org":"AWS","type":"architecture","title":"Backoff alone still makes clients retry in lockstep; jitter fixes it","blocks":["retry"],"flagship":false,"date":"2015-03-04","year":2015,"dateText":"4 Mar 2015","system":"A simulation by Marc Brooker of many clients contending for the same resource (an optimistic-concurrency write) and retrying when they lose.","happened":"With plain exponential backoff, failed clients wait 1, 2, 4, 8 ... units, all on the same schedule, so their retries still arrive in clusters. Adding randomness, for example \"Full Jitter\" (wait a random time between zero and the backoff), spreads the retries into a roughly constant rate.","fix":"The post recommends jitter (Full Jitter for least work); the Builders' Library card shows how Amazon applies it.","lesson":"Backoff decides how long to wait; jitter decides that not everyone waits the same amount.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"100","t":"contending clients in the simulation","q":3},{"v":"more than half","t":"fewer calls with jitter than with plain backoff","q":3}],"terms":[["Full Jitter","sleep = random(0, min(cap, base x 2^attempt))"],["optimistic concurrency","write only if nobody else changed the record since you read it; otherwise retry"]],"source":"AWS Architecture Blog, Exponential Backoff And Jitter (Marc Brooker)","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/exponential-backoff-and-jitter/","url2":null,"published":"2015-03-04","publishedText":"4 Mar 2015","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["It's obvious that the exponential backoff is working, in that the calls are happening less and less frequently.","The problem also stands out: there are still clusters of calls.","The gaps are gone, and beyond the initial spike, there's an approximately constant rate of calls.","In the case with 100 contending clients, we've reduced our call count by more than half.","The “Full Jitter” approach uses less work, but slightly more time."]},{"id":"twitter-2013","org":"Twitter","type":"architecture","title":"A peak 25 times the average: from a Ruby monolith to services","blocks":["lb","queue","retry"],"flagship":false,"date":"2013-08-03","year":2013,"dateText":"3 Aug 2013","system":"Twitter, which in 2013 took in more than 500 million Tweets a day.","happened":"On 3 August 2013 a TV airing in Japan produced a one-second peak of 143,199 Tweets per second, against an average of about 5,700. Earlier, Twitter had run one of the largest Ruby on Rails apps, where each process handled one request at a time and the answer was to throw more machines at it. It had since moved to services on the JVM (Tweet, timeline and user first), each scaled and tuned independently. On the way it learned that services had no consistent back-pressure signal and that clients aggressively retrying slow services caused thundering herds. During the peak users \"didn't experience a blip\".","fix":"Service-oriented architecture with independent capacity per service.","lesson":"Size for the peak, not the average; and know your peak-to-average ratio.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"143,199","t":"Tweets in one second, peak","q":0},{"v":"5,700","t":"Tweets per second on average","q":1},{"v":"25x","t":"peak over average","f":"143,199 / 5,700"}],"terms":[["monolith","one big application deployed as a unit"],["peak-to-average ratio","how much bigger the busiest second is than a typical second"]],"source":"Twitter Engineering blog, New Tweets per second record, and how! (read from the Wayback Machine)","url":"https://blog.twitter.com/engineering/en_us/a/2013/new-tweets-per-second-record-and-how","url2":null,"published":"2013-08-16","publishedText":"16 Aug 2013","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["Recently, something remarkable happened on Twitter: On Saturday, August 3 in Japan, people watched an airing of Castle in the Sky , and at one moment they took to Twitter so much that we hit a one-second peak of 143,199 Tweets per second.","To give you some context of how that compares to typical numbers, we normally take in more than 500 million Tweets a day which means about 5,700 Tweets a second, on average.","We were “throwing machines at the problem” instead of engineering thorough solutions –– our front-end Ruby machines were not handling the number of transactions per second that we thought was reasonable, given their horsepower.","Independent systems The largest architectural change we made was to move from our monolithic Ruby application to one that is more services oriented.","During this spike, our users didn’t experience a blip on Twitter.","We focused first on creating Tweet, timeline, and user services –– our “core nouns”.","For example, the failure semantics from clients to services didn’t interact well: we had no consistent back-pressure mechanism for servers to signal back to clients and we experienced “thundering herds” from clients aggressively retrying latent services."]},{"id":"memcache-2013","org":"Facebook","type":"architecture","title":"Leases cut a cache stampede from 17,000 to 1,300 queries a second","blocks":["cache"],"flagship":false,"date":"2013-04-02","year":2013,"dateText":"2 Apr 2013","system":"Facebook put memcached, an in-memory cache, in front of MySQL: the largest memcached installation known, at over a billion requests per second.","happened":"When a hot key is deleted (because its data changed), every web server that misses it queries the database at once: a thundering herd. Facebook added leases: on a miss, the cache gives one client a token to refill the key (at most one token per key every 10 seconds) and tells the others to wait a short time and retry the cache, by which point the value is usually there. Leases also stop a slow client from writing an old value over a newer one (a stale set).","fix":"With leases, the peak database query rate fell from 17K/s to 1.3K/s in their measurement.","lesson":"On a cache miss, let one caller refill and make the rest wait.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"17K/s","t":"peak database queries without leases","q":2},{"v":"1.3K/s","t":"with leases","q":3},{"v":"13x","t":"fewer peak queries","f":"17,000 / 1,300"}],"terms":[["cache-aside","the app reads the cache; on a miss it reads the database and writes the answer into the cache"],["thundering herd","many clients missing the same key and hitting the database together"]],"source":"Nishtala et al., Scaling Memcache at Facebook, NSDI 2013 (USENIX)","url":"https://www.usenix.org/system/files/conference/nsdi13/nsdi13-final170_update.pdf","url2":null,"published":"2013-04","publishedText":"Apr 2013","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["To the best of our knowledge, this system is the largest memcached installation in the world, processing over a billion requests per second and storing trillions of items.","We introduce a new mechanism we call leases to address two problems: stale sets and thundering herds.","Without leases, all of the cache misses resulted in a peak database query rate of 17K/s.","With leases, the peak database query rate was 1.3K/s.","By default, we configure these servers to return a token only once every 10 seconds per key.","Requests for a key’s value within 10 seconds of a token being issued results in a special notification telling the client to wait a short amount of time."]},{"id":"insta-2012","org":"Instagram","type":"architecture","title":"Thousands of logical shards on a few Postgres servers","blocks":["shard"],"flagship":false,"date":"2012-12-30","year":2012,"dateText":"30 Dec 2012","system":"Instagram stored photos, likes and users in PostgreSQL, and needed IDs that were unique across shards and sortable by time.","happened":"Data is split by user ID into thousands of logical shards (Postgres schemas), many per physical server. To grow, a set of logical shards moves to a new server; no data is re-bucketed. Each ID is built inside the database: 41 bits of milliseconds since a custom epoch, 13 bits of shard ID, 10 bits of a per-table sequence.","fix":"IDs sort by time without a central ID service, and the shard is readable from the ID itself.","lesson":"Split into many small logical shards early; moving them later is cheap, re-splitting is not.","note":"Checked: 2^41 milliseconds is about 69.7 years, not the 41 years the post states (recomputed in recompute.py). The design is unaffected; the figure in the post is a slip.","numbers":[{"v":"41 bits","t":"milliseconds since a custom epoch","q":1},{"v":"13 bits","t":"logical shard ID","q":1},{"v":"10 bits","t":"sequence, modulus 1024","q":1},{"v":"2000","t":"logical shards in their worked example","q":2},{"v":"1024","t":"IDs per shard per millisecond","f":"2^10"}],"terms":[["shard","one slice of the data, stored on its own server, chosen by a key such as user ID"],["logical shard","a slice that is smaller than a server, so several live on one machine and can be moved"]],"source":"Instagram Engineering, Sharding & IDs at Instagram (read from the Wayback Machine; Medium blocks scripts)","url":"https://instagram-engineering.com/sharding-ids-at-instagram-1cf5a71e5a5c","url2":null,"published":"2012-12-30","publishedText":"30 Dec 2012","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["With more than 25 photos and 90 likes every second, we store a lot of data here at Instagram.","Each of our IDs consists of: 41 bits for time in milliseconds (gives us 41 years of IDs with a custom epoch) 13 bits that represent the logical shard ID 10 bits that represent an auto-incrementing sequence, modulus 1024.","Let's say we're sharding by user ID, and there are 2000 logical shards; if our user ID is 31341, then the shard ID is 31341 % 2000 -> 1341.","Using this approach, we can start with just a few database servers, and eventually move to many more, simply by moving a set of logical shards from one database to another, without having to re-bucket any of our data.","We used Postgres’ schemas feature to make this easy to script and administrate."]},{"id":"knight-2012-08","org":"Knight Capital","type":"outage","title":"Seven of eight servers got the new code; the eighth lost $460 million","blocks":["deploy"],"flagship":false,"date":"2012-08-01","year":2012,"dateText":"1 Aug 2012","system":"Knight's order router SMARS ran on eight servers and turned client orders into many smaller orders sent to the stock exchange.","happened":"New code reused an old flag that once switched on a dead feature called Power Peg. A technician copied the new code to only seven of the eight servers. On the eighth, the reused flag woke the old code, which sent orders without ever stopping. Nothing checked that all eight servers ran the same code, and Knight had no procedure to halt the system in response to its own aberrant activity.","fix":"The SEC fined Knight $12 million and found it lacked controls to ensure the orderly deployment of new code, to prevent old code from being activated, and to halt the system.","lesson":"Deploys must be automated and verified on every machine, and dead code with live switches must be removed.","note":null,"numbers":[{"v":"eight","t":"servers; one was missed by the deploy","q":0},{"v":"45 minutes","t":"of runaway trading","q":2},{"v":"4 million","t":"executions in 154 stocks","q":2},{"v":"$460 million","t":"loss","q":3}],"terms":[["kill switch","a control that stops a system immediately, without a new deploy"],["dead code","code still shipped but no longer meant to run"]],"source":"US Securities and Exchange Commission, Administrative Proceeding File No. 3-15570, In the Matter of Knight Capital Americas LLC (Release No. 70694)","url":"https://www.sec.gov/files/litigation/admin/2013/34-70694.pdf","url2":null,"published":"2013-10-16","publishedText":"16 Oct 2013","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["During the deployment of the new code, however, one of Knight's technicians did not copy the new code to one of the eight SMARS computer servers.","However, orders sent with the repurposed flag to the eighth server triggered the defective Power Peg code still present on that server.","For the 212 incoming parent orders that were processed by the defective Power Peg code, SMARS sent millions of child orders, resulting in 4 million executions in 154 stocks for more than 397 million shares in approximately 45 minutes.","Ultimately, Knight realized a $460 million loss on these positions.","Knight also did not have procedures in place to halt SMARS's operations in response to its own aberrant activity.","Pursuant to Section 21B(a)(1) and (2) of the Exchange Act, Respondent Knight shall, within ten (10) days of the entry of this Order, pay a civil money penalty in the amount of $12,000,000 ($12 million) to the United States Treasury."]},{"id":"netflix-2011","org":"Netflix","type":"architecture","title":"Chaos Monkey: break production on purpose, during office hours","blocks":["degrade","obs"],"flagship":false,"date":"2011-07-19","year":2011,"dateText":"19 Jul 2011","system":"Netflix's streaming service on AWS, built from many services on many instances.","happened":"Netflix built Chaos Monkey, which randomly kills production instances, and ran it during business hours with engineers watching, so that every service had to survive losing a machine. Its siblings inject latency (Latency Monkey) or simulate losing a whole availability zone (Chaos Gorilla).","fix":"Practising failures in daylight let teams build automatic recovery for them before a real one arrived.","lesson":"If you never practise failure, your first practice is the real outage.","note":null,"numbers":[],"terms":[["availability zone","one or more data centers in a region with separate power and network, so they rarely fail together"],["chaos engineering","injecting failures deliberately to check the system copes"]],"source":"Netflix Tech Blog, The Netflix Simian Army (read from the Wayback Machine)","url":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","url2":"https://web.archive.org/web/2015/http://techblog.netflix.com/2011/07/netflix-simian-army.html","published":"2011-07-19","publishedText":"19 Jul 2011","accessed":"2026-10-04","quotes":["This was our philosophy when we built Chaos Monkey , a tool that randomly disables our production instances to make sure we can survive this common type of failure without any customer impact.","By running Chaos Monkey in the middle of a business day, in a carefully monitored environment with engineers standing by to address any problems, we can still learn the lessons about the weaknesses of our system, and build automatic recovery mechanisms to deal with them.","Latency Monkey induces artificial delays in our RESTful client-server communication layer to simulate service degradation and measures if upstream services respond appropriately.","Chaos Gorilla is similar to Chaos Monkey, but simulates an outage of an entire Amazon availability zone."]}],"accessed":"2026-10-04"};
